"""
# Implicit-Self Field Assignment — corrected contract

This file used to be a CHARACTERIZATION spec for
`doc/08_tracking/bug/interp_implicit_self_field_assignment_silent_noop_2026-07-17.md`.
It asserted the buggy behaviour on purpose: inside a `me` method, a bare
`flag = true` silently no-opped, so the spec pinned `probe.flag == false`
and was written to fail loudly the moment the bug was fixed.

The bug is now fixed, so that pinning has been removed rather than left
stale --- exactly the update the old docstring asked for.

The corrected contract is: a bare `field = value` inside a method body is a
HARD ERROR naming the shadowed field, instead of minting a fresh local and
discarding the mutation. That negative case cannot be exercised from inside
this spec, because the error aborts the program under test; it is covered by
driving the interpreter as a subprocess in
`test/01_unit/compiler/interpreter/implicit_self_field_assign_spec.spl`.

What remains here is the positive system-level guarantee that the documented
form --- `self.field = value`, per `.claude/memory/ref_coding.md` --- keeps
mutating the receiver. ("Implicit self" means omitting `self` from the
*parameter list*, never from field access.)
"""

use std.spec

class ImplicitSelfProbe:
    flag: bool

    me set_via_explicit_self():
        self.flag = true

describe "explicit self.field assignment inside a me method":
    """
    Guards the form the language actually specifies: an explicit
    `self.field = value` inside a `me` method mutates the receiver.
    """

    it "explicit self.field assignment mutates the field":
        var probe = ImplicitSelfProbe(flag: false)
        probe.set_via_explicit_self()
        assert_true(probe.flag)

    it "leaves an untouched receiver at its constructed value":
        var probe = ImplicitSelfProbe(flag: false)
        assert_true(probe.flag == false)
